WEBVTT

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:01.980
Yakitate!! 9!

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:03.730
The hostilities began at Ooma, Aomori.

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:07.570
The opponent is Tsubodzuka Takurni from CMAP,

00:00:07.570 --> 00:00:09.820
an idol group of serious cook-entertainers.

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:12.650
His skill was the real thing.

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:17.990
Super tuna, the specialty of Ooma,
was all bought up by Yukino.

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:21.660
So, Kazuma and the gang take on the challenge
with chawanmushi-bread made with sea urchin.

00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:24.120
As the most important requirement for the match,

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:29.800
Kazuma requests Kawachi to make bad
French bread that is high in water content.

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:32.510
No! This is not good! Not good!

00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:34.090
Wait... What is it?

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:35.800
I don’t want the present Kawachi.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:37.970
Please go back to the way you used to be!

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:41.560
But, what Kazuma did with the French bread was...

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:43.810
Huh? What are you talking about?

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:47.070
I wouldn’t use this crappy bread
just like that.

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:49.070
How harsh! But, what was that?!

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:51.030
I take this bread...

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:52.690
do this...

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:55.420
and then it goes into the
garbage bag for burnable trash!

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:04.370
There’s English bread, German bread, and French bread,

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:06.250
but Japan’s bread, <i>Japan</i>, does not exist.

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:08.630
In that case, there’s no choice but to create it.

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:12.050
This story is a serious, biographical ballad of a boy who possesses Solar Hands,

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:17.090
Azuma Kazuma, who will create a Japanese bread made by and for the Japanese people,

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:21.140
which can be presented to the world proudly.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:54.490
Awaken!!

00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:56.460
Super Kuroyanagi!

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:01.030
All right!

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:03.580
A-Are you trying to harass me?

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:06.290
You made me bake that bread just to throw it away?!

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:08.130
I won’t throw it away.

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:10.380
I’ll be sure to use it after improving on it.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:11.380
Don’t worry.

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:13.300
B-But...

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:16.930
If you do that, the already soggy bread
will absorb the steam inside the bag and...

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:19.190
Become even soggier, right?

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:22.590
But, that’s what I’m aiming for.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:25.550
Judge!

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:27.690
He’s crazy!

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:30.400
There’s no meaning to a nonsense battle like this!

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:31.890
That’s not true.

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:36.280
His motive may be beyond
the grasp of rationality, but...

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:37.440
that’s his style.

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:39.910
His skill is the real thing.

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:41.450
I can guarantee that.

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:43.820
If you understand, then get back to your spot quickly!

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:48.130
It can’t be helped.

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:52.790
Not even I have the slightest idea as to what he’s trying to do.

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:55.920
It’s the Fresh Garbage <i>Japan</i>!

00:03:57.090 --> 00:03:59.300
Please do spare me that though, Azuma.

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:07.350
All right!

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:08.900
I guess this should do it.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:11.270
The bread I made is...

00:04:11.270 --> 00:04:12.480
Hey, Azuma...

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:13.650
You’re really going to use that?

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:14.570
Yep, it’s fine.

00:04:14.570 --> 00:04:16.240
Thank you, Kawachi.

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:18.110
It’s all thanks to you.

00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:20.530
You’re a genius when it comes to making bad bread.

00:04:20.530 --> 00:04:23.900
Looks like you’ve finally come to understand my skill!

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:28.910
There’s no one who can
compare to me at making bad bread!

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:31.920
I was complimented and yet my tears flow.

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:34.920
Geez, this guy’s really in the way.

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:35.670
All right!

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:39.170
So, first we cut this into two halves.

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:40.880
Scoop out the insides.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:42.880
All that’s left is to beat it with this.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:44.520
A hammer?

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:46.390
What in the world is Azuma-san...?

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:51.900
If Tsubodzuka-san’s skill is the
real thing like you said earlier, then...

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:53.480
Somehow it’s hard to believe that we can win...

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:55.520
I wonder...

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:58.150
It’s true that Tsubodzuka is strong.

00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:02.030
But, Azuma is about to do
something amazing in his own way.

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:03.530
Something amazing...?

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:04.950
Yeah.

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:06.030
You see, Azuma is...

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:09.370
trying to breathe life into garbage!

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:10.620
Life, you say?!

00:05:11.170 --> 00:05:12.210
Kawachi-san!

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:14.210
We’re still in the middle of the match,
so why are you with the audience?!

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:21.210
Kanmuri suggested that I rest among the audience
and save up on my genius for the next match.

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:23.560
He was probably just in the way.

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:24.420
Yep.

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:29.220
All right, now!

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:34.070
These past 10 years,

00:05:34.070 --> 00:05:37.450
the training and competitions I’ve gone through to become a member of CMAP

00:05:37.450 --> 00:05:38.980
were not just over cooking.

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:42.000
In the end, we’re entertainers.

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:45.200
In order to raise the viewership,

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:49.740
we had to act like idiots and learn specific
ways of speaking in order to be accepted.

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:52.340
It was humiliating.

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:55.710
The character that was chosen for me

00:05:55.710 --> 00:05:58.250
was that of the “mysterious boy” who uses a lot of foreign words.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:02.460
Thanks to that, I can no longer
talk like a regular person.

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:04.390
I love cooking.

00:06:04.390 --> 00:06:09.470
I wanted to tell the world about how much fun cooking is by becoming an entertainer.

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:13.970
Without that sincere purpose,
I couldn’t have made it this far.

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:22.070
That’s why I absolutely cannot accept a
cook like you who’s just fooling around.

00:06:22.070 --> 00:06:24.610
I’ll be sure to teach you and the
world exactly what real cooking is.

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:28.490
To you and the world
I will teach it exactly!
I’ll be sure to teach you and the
world exactly what real cooking is.

00:06:33.670 --> 00:06:34.460
What’s happening?!

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:36.830
There’s something fantastic happening in Tsubodzuka’s oven.

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:39.850
Won’t it just burn?

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:43.180
It’s done.

00:06:43.180 --> 00:06:46.100
This is a bread suitable for this place, Ooma.

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:48.050
It’s Super Toro Aburi.
Note: toro = fatty portion of the tuna; aburi = broiled

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:49.800
Oh, this is...

00:06:51.020 --> 00:06:53.800
You first take the pastry out of the oven,

00:06:55.230 --> 00:06:57.360
use avocado paste to attach the tuna to it,

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:00.810
and return it to the oven
and broil it for a moment, right?

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:06.620
When you broil tuna, the fatty
portion will rise up to the surface,

00:07:06.620 --> 00:07:11.090
so that when you put it in your mouth,
it has a richer flavor than raw tuna.

00:07:11.410 --> 00:07:15.080
So that’s what the flames in the oven were for...

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:17.790
But, this bread still has a...

00:07:17.790 --> 00:07:18.950
Judge.

00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:23.710
Please eat it as soon as possible.

00:07:23.710 --> 00:07:25.940
Yeah, I know.

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:35.310
I-It can’t be...

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:39.180
That broiled bread is good enough to awaken him?!

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:44.820
He’s evolved.

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:46.990
He’s no longer the Kuroyanagi he’s been so far.

00:07:46.990 --> 00:07:48.910
Th-Then... what in the world is he?!

00:07:48.910 --> 00:07:50.490
Super Toro Aburi!

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:51.620
Super Kuro Aburi!

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:52.410
Super Kuroyaburi!

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:54.830
Super Kuroyanagi!

00:07:54.830 --> 00:07:56.660
What was that?!

00:07:56.660 --> 00:08:01.010
Super Kuroyanagi!!

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:09.850
J... Just as expected of CMAP.

00:08:09.850 --> 00:08:11.680
This excellent work has made me...

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:13.480
no, has really touched me.

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:16.390
All this just for having broiled some measly tuna?

00:08:17.020 --> 00:08:19.060
While you may call yourself Super Kuroyanagi,

00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:22.640
it looks like you might have just become
a guy with cheap two-bit reactions.

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:25.150
What was that?!

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:41.800
What is this?!

00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:44.370
Why have I become a freezer?!

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:45.170
Freezer

00:08:45.170 --> 00:08:46.330
Also... Where is this?
Freezer

00:08:46.330 --> 00:08:47.420
Also... Where is this?

00:08:51.050 --> 00:08:52.560
Super Kuroyanagi!!

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:54.060
Hey, Freezer-boy!

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:56.930
You said that I’m displaying
cheap two-bit reactions, right?

00:08:56.930 --> 00:08:58.650
Y... Yeah, what about it?!

00:08:58.650 --> 00:09:01.230
In that case, I’ll show you...

00:09:01.230 --> 00:09:03.640
just how amazing this bread is.

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:10.570
While this bread is called Super Toro Aburi...

00:09:10.570 --> 00:09:12.440
its true worth doesn’t lie in the broiled tuna!

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:21.870
It’s the amazing dough that
was worthy enough to convert me.

00:09:22.340 --> 00:09:24.120
Freezer Hurricane!

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:25.880
The legendary dough...

00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:28.560
The famed beehive pastry!

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:41.980
Beehive pastry?

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:43.730
Freezer-boy!

00:09:43.730 --> 00:09:45.980
If you’re to compare sashimi to grilled fish...

00:09:45.980 --> 00:09:49.980
you know that it’s harder to cut, right?

00:09:55.870 --> 00:09:59.460
It’s common sense for a cook to know that
raw stuff is harder to cut than cooked stuff!

00:09:59.460 --> 00:10:03.450
In that case, what would you do
to make sashimi go with bread?

00:10:08.590 --> 00:10:12.640
Unlike rice, bread doesn’t fall apart in your mouth.

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:14.560
So, it’s hard to chew on.

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:21.050
In that case, pastry dough such as what
Tsubodzuka made or pie dough would be...

00:10:24.020 --> 00:10:25.270
Exactly!

00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:29.820
In that case, what would you do
to make the dough crisp?

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:30.560
I don’t know.

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:32.400
You mix in choux dough!

00:10:40.910 --> 00:10:45.670
By mixing in the fluffy and airy choux creme dough,

00:10:45.670 --> 00:10:49.960
you can create hollow spaces within the dough,
giving you a pastry that resembles a beehive...

00:10:51.220 --> 00:10:52.470
And that is...

00:10:52.470 --> 00:10:53.970
this...

00:10:53.970 --> 00:10:57.420
beehive pastry!

00:11:13.660 --> 00:11:16.320
What in the world... have I...

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:22.120
This beehive pastry is made of the ultimate dough,
combining the powers of pastry and choux dough.

00:11:22.120 --> 00:11:24.620
But... if you mess up the ratio,

00:11:24.620 --> 00:11:28.590
there will be too much air inside,
leading to it falling apart easily.

00:11:28.590 --> 00:11:30.840
So, there’s not many people who take up the challenge.

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:33.960
Therefore, it has been
considered legendary for a long time.

00:11:34.340 --> 00:11:36.300
How can this be...

00:11:36.300 --> 00:11:37.920
This is an amazing technique.

00:11:38.930 --> 00:11:42.890
It looks like you’ve finally understood my strength, and that makes me very happy.

00:11:42.890 --> 00:11:45.930
You guys didn’t have a chance
of winning from the very start.

00:11:46.520 --> 00:11:48.770
I guess it’s to be expected of CMAP.

00:11:48.770 --> 00:11:49.570
Yeah.

00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:51.440
What are you guys so relaxed about?!

00:11:52.150 --> 00:11:54.450
Since I made it, I know best.

00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:59.030
No matter what, it’s impossible to
make that awful bread even look good.

00:11:59.030 --> 00:12:00.910
All right. We’re...

00:12:00.910 --> 00:12:01.820
Done, too!

00:12:03.330 --> 00:12:08.040
This is my Ooma <i>Japan</i>,
sea urchin chawanmushi-bread!

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:20.050
This is my Ooma <i>Japan</i>,
sea urchin chawanmushi-bread!

00:12:20.050 --> 00:12:23.430
Oh, that looks like a really delicious chawanmushi!

00:12:23.430 --> 00:12:27.020
And it seems to be in a wonderful and proper Japanese-style bowl.

00:12:27.020 --> 00:12:28.610
It’s wonderful.

00:12:28.610 --> 00:12:31.310
I wonder what famous artist created that.

00:12:31.310 --> 00:12:34.310
But, where in the world is the bread?

00:12:34.310 --> 00:12:39.460
No matter how delicious a chawanmushi it is,
if there’s no bread, it’s not much of a match.

00:12:40.030 --> 00:12:41.700
What are you talking about?

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:45.040
Don’t you even remember
the bread you made yourself?

00:12:45.040 --> 00:12:49.210
That wonderful and proper Japanese-style bowl is the crappy bread that you made!

00:12:49.210 --> 00:12:50.830
What was that?!

00:12:52.630 --> 00:12:54.340
This looks wonderful.

00:12:54.340 --> 00:12:58.170
A really delicious chawanmushi
topped with urchin that shines brilliantly.

00:12:58.170 --> 00:13:01.380
And this wonderful bread-bowl!

00:13:01.970 --> 00:13:03.380
A bread-bowl?!

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:07.690
Normally, when you make
bread that’s to hold some fluid,

00:13:07.690 --> 00:13:11.820
you have to deep-fry the bread in
order to prevent it from getting soggy.

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:14.850
So, you can’t prevent it from getting greasy.

00:13:14.850 --> 00:13:17.490
But, you’ve not used oil for this bread.

00:13:17.490 --> 00:13:20.570
Exactly what magic did you use for this?!

00:13:20.570 --> 00:13:22.450
That’s simple.

00:13:22.450 --> 00:13:26.910
First I scooped out the insides of the bad bread, then I beat it with a hammer.

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:28.290
I see!

00:13:28.290 --> 00:13:32.960
If you beat it with a hammer, the pores of the bread are closed so that fluid cannot seep in.

00:13:32.960 --> 00:13:35.120
So, you no longer need to fry it.

00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:36.870
That’s it.

00:13:36.870 --> 00:13:41.420
But, if you beat a regular bread
with a hammer, it would break right?

00:13:41.890 --> 00:13:47.130
That’s why I put it in a garbage bag and
allowed it to absorb plenty of moisture.

00:13:47.130 --> 00:13:51.640
And it’s for the same reason you made Kawachi bake that horrible soggy bread?

00:13:51.640 --> 00:13:53.860
Well, that’s true, but...

00:13:53.860 --> 00:13:56.640
That’s not the only reason.

00:13:56.640 --> 00:13:59.400
Dough which is high in moisture takes time to bake.

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:03.360
So, the crust becomes thicker, right?

00:14:03.360 --> 00:14:06.790
So, when you stretch the dough
by beating it, it’s less likely to break.

00:14:06.790 --> 00:14:09.040
It has a deeper flavor, too!

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:10.670
I see...

00:14:10.670 --> 00:14:13.870
That’s a really amazing trick, Azuma!

00:14:14.790 --> 00:14:15.580
Is that so...?

00:14:16.220 --> 00:14:18.330
But, there’s just one thing that’s bothering me...

00:14:19.220 --> 00:14:21.590
Where did the insides that you scooped out go?

00:14:21.590 --> 00:14:23.680
They’re not lying around there...

00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:25.090
And it doesn’t look like they’ve been thrown away either.

00:14:25.090 --> 00:14:26.770
Hey...

00:14:26.770 --> 00:14:30.730
You’ve become Super-Kuro now,
so eat it super-fast will you?

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:32.020
It’ll get cold, right?

00:14:32.020 --> 00:14:33.600
Don’t abbreviate!

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:35.480
But it’s so long...

00:14:35.480 --> 00:14:38.440
Old Super Kuroyanagi... I mean Mister.

00:14:39.820 --> 00:14:41.320
Oh whatever.

00:14:41.320 --> 00:14:41.940
Well then...

00:14:52.950 --> 00:14:56.830
It looks like that crazy action of his
had some meaning behind it.

00:15:00.090 --> 00:15:04.970
It’s not possible for a person like you
to make something truly delicious.

00:15:04.970 --> 00:15:08.590
And the judge’s reaction will be sure to show that.

00:15:14.980 --> 00:15:17.060
To think that there’s no reaction!

00:15:18.270 --> 00:15:19.600
The winner is Pantasia!

00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:20.730
We did it!

00:15:20.730 --> 00:15:21.690
What was that?!

00:15:21.690 --> 00:15:22.730
Yay.

00:15:24.950 --> 00:15:26.780
I object!

00:15:26.780 --> 00:15:30.490
I made a bread good enough to awaken you!

00:15:30.490 --> 00:15:33.960
It’s unbelievable that a no-reaction bread could win!

00:15:33.960 --> 00:15:39.200
You probably made that judgment because
you’re a former Pantasia member!

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:44.180
W-Well, someone who
produced all that big a reaction...

00:15:44.180 --> 00:15:45.550
What are you talking about?

00:15:45.550 --> 00:15:47.300
Look carefully!

00:15:47.300 --> 00:15:49.550
It’s not like he didn’t have a reaction!

00:15:49.550 --> 00:15:53.050
It’s hard to notice because
he’s already Super Kuroyanagi,

00:15:53.050 --> 00:15:55.300
but he’s matured...

00:15:55.300 --> 00:15:57.760
no, evolved even further!

00:15:59.270 --> 00:16:00.770
Th-This is...

00:16:03.190 --> 00:16:04.610
Sea urchin!

00:16:04.610 --> 00:16:08.410
Each and every one of Kuro-yan’s hairs is spiked, and he’s turned into a sea-urchin!

00:16:08.410 --> 00:16:10.190
In other words, that form is...

00:16:10.190 --> 00:16:13.200
Super Kuroyanagi II.

00:16:13.200 --> 00:16:15.200
T-Two...?

00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:17.540
Remember this well, Kawachi.

00:16:17.540 --> 00:16:20.750
While Kuroyanagi is a moron,
a show-off and a piece of shit...

00:16:20.750 --> 00:16:24.960
he’s not a coward who will
show favoritism while judging!

00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:27.210
That’s saying too much, Afro!

00:16:29.140 --> 00:16:31.510
Since you were mouthing off,
his hair became even more...

00:16:31.510 --> 00:16:33.630
Super Kuroyanagi III, huh?

00:16:33.630 --> 00:16:35.390
Listen up, Tsubodzuka.

00:16:36.020 --> 00:16:40.530
No matter what you think, I do not
show favoritism when it comes to food!

00:16:41.190 --> 00:16:42.280
Hmph!

00:16:42.280 --> 00:16:44.820
Then, let me explain it to you until you can accept it...

00:16:44.820 --> 00:16:47.980
All the magic tricks hidden within Azuma’s bread.

00:16:49.160 --> 00:16:52.540
First, the bowl made by beating the bread.

00:16:52.540 --> 00:16:56.920
The point of it not soaking up liquid
has already been explained.

00:16:56.920 --> 00:17:00.880
But, by beating it, there’s one more
advantage hidden within this bread.

00:17:00.880 --> 00:17:02.250
And that is...

00:17:03.340 --> 00:17:08.920
By flattening the crust, it concentrates the flavor!

00:17:08.920 --> 00:17:13.430
The Italian panini is famous for
taking advantage of this principle.

00:17:13.430 --> 00:17:15.560
But, it’s the same for French bread.

00:17:15.560 --> 00:17:20.430
By pressing it while baking, the
crust has a deeper flavor than before.

00:17:21.310 --> 00:17:23.570
Next, the chawanmushi.

00:17:23.570 --> 00:17:26.940
The taste of the egg used here was truly splendid.

00:17:26.940 --> 00:17:28.560
The taste of the egg?

00:17:28.560 --> 00:17:32.150
This egg was probably chosen by Kanmuri, right?

00:17:32.150 --> 00:17:34.990
Oh, how did you know?

00:17:34.990 --> 00:17:40.660
You’re the only one in that group erudite enough to be able to choose such an ingredient.

00:17:40.660 --> 00:17:43.040
Oh, you exaggerate.

00:17:43.880 --> 00:17:44.790
Tsubodzuka.

00:17:45.630 --> 00:17:49.880
What they used is the pride of
Aomori prefecture, the high-quality egg,

00:17:50.840 --> 00:17:52.380
Shindofuji.

00:17:53.260 --> 00:17:54.380
Shindofuji?!

00:17:54.380 --> 00:17:59.640
Shindofuji is produced by hens that are raised
with particular attention to their environment.

00:18:00.650 --> 00:18:02.560
In order to have them produce large numbers of eggs,

00:18:02.560 --> 00:18:06.110
the drinking water of the hens is underground
mineral water from Mt. Hakkouda.

00:18:06.110 --> 00:18:09.850
Their feed is all organic vegetables and includes herbs.

00:18:09.850 --> 00:18:12.730
Underground water, organic vegetables and herbs?!

00:18:14.080 --> 00:18:16.990
Talk about pampered hens...

00:18:16.990 --> 00:18:22.370
Chawanmushi is said to be a dish
that begins and ends with the eggs used.

00:18:22.920 --> 00:18:25.420
So, I searched for and brought
these eggs as a secret weapon

00:18:25.420 --> 00:18:31.210
from Kamikita district, Aomori
prefecture, not far from Ooma.

00:18:32.090 --> 00:18:36.420
You used avocado to bind the
bread and the super tuna together.

00:18:36.420 --> 00:18:39.930
But that’s not cultivated around here at all, is it?

00:18:41.020 --> 00:18:45.310
Considering the theme,
that may have been a little weak.

00:18:46.440 --> 00:18:48.690
I have no choice but to
acknowledge your advantage there.

00:18:48.690 --> 00:18:52.740
But, my advantage cannot be defeated by just that!

00:18:52.740 --> 00:18:57.190
In the first place, the only
bread portion in that is the bowl.

00:18:57.190 --> 00:19:00.660
The advantage that it has as a bread is zero!

00:19:02.160 --> 00:19:05.500
All your excuses are bullshit!

00:19:05.500 --> 00:19:08.000
And I’ll know that immediately after eating this!

00:19:12.830 --> 00:19:15.180
Wh... What’s with him?!

00:19:15.180 --> 00:19:18.380
He’s stopped moving after
putting Azuma’s bread in his mouth!

00:19:22.720 --> 00:19:24.300
More importantly, Kawachi-san...

00:19:24.300 --> 00:19:25.850
Doesn’t that hurt?

00:19:25.850 --> 00:19:27.350
Huh? What?

00:19:27.350 --> 00:19:29.350
Ouch!

00:19:29.350 --> 00:19:33.070
Ow ow ow ow ow ow ow.

00:19:33.070 --> 00:19:34.530
I’ve suddenly grown hair!

00:19:34.530 --> 00:19:35.650
No!

00:19:35.650 --> 00:19:39.160
I’ve suddenly been stabbed by hairs!

00:19:39.160 --> 00:19:40.570
Could it be that...?

00:19:41.330 --> 00:19:43.070
H-Hair needles?!

00:19:43.070 --> 00:19:45.470
So delicious that it makes your hair fly out, huh?

00:19:46.210 --> 00:19:48.330
Nice reaction, Tsubodzuka.

00:19:49.130 --> 00:19:51.170
Now you understand, right?

00:19:51.170 --> 00:19:53.330
What’s truly fearsome about that bread.

00:19:53.330 --> 00:19:56.010
At the beginning, I was mistaken, too.

00:19:56.010 --> 00:20:00.090
The most praiseworthy trick applied to this bread is...

00:20:00.620 --> 00:20:02.090
the dough inside!

00:20:02.090 --> 00:20:04.190
The dough inside?!

00:20:04.890 --> 00:20:07.610
Why are you getting surprised, Kawachi-san?

00:20:08.060 --> 00:20:12.100
Well, they didn’t explain anything to me.

00:20:13.650 --> 00:20:16.940
It’s not like Azuma threw away the
insides of the bread after scooping them out.

00:20:17.450 --> 00:20:20.120
He had Kanmuri dice the bread inside very small,

00:20:20.120 --> 00:20:22.950
and then stir-fry it with sea urchin,

00:20:22.950 --> 00:20:25.160
like croutons.

00:20:25.870 --> 00:20:28.910
When bread is stir-fried, the
moisture content within is lowered.

00:20:28.910 --> 00:20:32.410
Especially with breads already high in moisture.

00:20:33.090 --> 00:20:37.170
This is another reason that Azuma
was so particular about the crappy bread.

00:20:37.170 --> 00:20:43.350
And the inner bread is still living
within the bowl as urchin-croutons.

00:20:43.350 --> 00:20:49.510
In other words, more than half of this
chawanmushi was marvelously delicious bread.

00:20:49.510 --> 00:20:54.700
So, don’t you think that this has
plenty of advantage as a bread?

00:20:55.570 --> 00:20:57.290
I raise the white flag.

00:20:57.290 --> 00:20:58.780
Yay.

00:21:01.450 --> 00:21:02.910
Tsubodzuka...

00:21:02.910 --> 00:21:06.080
I know that you judged
Azuma’s attitude to not be serious.

00:21:06.080 --> 00:21:08.750
But, that’s precisely the secret to his strength.

00:21:10.250 --> 00:21:12.500
Cooking is essentially
something you should have fun doing.

00:21:12.960 --> 00:21:15.710
Having fun while cooking together with your friends...

00:21:15.710 --> 00:21:18.910
Nothing can surpass that.

00:21:20.050 --> 00:21:23.520
I think that’s probably the
most important thing you’ve lost...

00:21:23.520 --> 00:21:26.770
as you continued to crush your companions...

00:21:26.770 --> 00:21:30.720
in order to survive in CMAP.

00:21:32.940 --> 00:21:35.730
How foolish I was.

00:21:37.400 --> 00:21:41.450
I worked hard to show how much fun cooking was to other people.

00:21:41.450 --> 00:21:43.750
I reached so far and yet...

00:21:44.240 --> 00:21:45.250
he still...

00:21:45.250 --> 00:21:48.420
To think that I lost the most important thing,

00:21:48.420 --> 00:21:53.420
a heart that enjoys cooking together with your friends...

00:21:56.260 --> 00:21:58.380
I lost, Azuma-kun.

00:21:58.380 --> 00:21:59.880
You are a real chef.

00:21:59.880 --> 00:22:00.960
I respect you.

00:22:01.760 --> 00:22:02.970
Tsubodzuka...

00:22:02.970 --> 00:22:04.970
And...

00:22:04.970 --> 00:22:07.020
If possible...

00:22:07.020 --> 00:22:09.810
As a fellow cook...

00:22:09.810 --> 00:22:10.970
Would you be...

00:22:13.230 --> 00:22:17.120
friends with me?

00:22:17.680 --> 00:22:18.870
Of course!

00:22:21.160 --> 00:22:23.450
Tsubodzuka-san looks really happy.

00:22:23.450 --> 00:22:25.320
All’s well that ends well.

00:22:26.120 --> 00:22:28.070
Who the hell cares.

00:22:28.790 --> 00:22:31.920
Please take these needles out immediately?

00:22:31.920 --> 00:22:33.430
Of the same opinion here.

00:24:05.530 --> 00:24:06.050
Huh?!

00:24:06.050 --> 00:24:06.890
Huh?! Huh?!

00:24:06.890 --> 00:24:07.550
This is strange...

00:24:07.550 --> 00:24:09.010
There’s something strange.

00:24:09.840 --> 00:24:11.610
I can’t have any reactions!

00:24:12.390 --> 00:24:14.150
Isn’t this kinda bad?!

00:24:14.150 --> 00:24:15.100
Wait!

00:24:15.100 --> 00:24:16.610
Next time, on Yakitate!! Japan,

00:24:16.610 --> 00:24:17.940
Kuroyanagi in danger!!

00:24:17.940 --> 00:24:19.360
The lost reactions!

00:24:19.360 --> 00:24:19.980
Be sure to watch.

00:24:20.490 --> 00:24:22.730
Yakitate!! Seed of knowledge.

00:24:23.200 --> 00:24:26.240
In addition to DHA, tuna has high quantities of EPA.

00:24:26.240 --> 00:24:28.080
EPA is eicosapentanoic acid.

00:24:28.080 --> 00:24:31.740
It reduces bad cholesterol and helps improve the flow of blood.

00:24:32.210 --> 00:24:34.460
Oh?
